"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[1773],{1773:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"testId":"medicine-test-2","slug":"medicine","subject":"Medicine","testNo":2,"durationMin":60,"questions":[{"n":1,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 53-year-old female patient presents with complaints of weakness for the last 2 months. Additionally, she is facing difficulty rising from a chair, and combing her hair every morning. A physical exam reveals decreased strength in the shoulders and hip. Skin findings are as shown in the image. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["SLE","Sjogren\'s syndrome","Dermatomyositis","Sarcoidosis"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Dermatomyositis\\n\\nIn the given clinical vignette, this patient\'s symptoms along with the\xa0image findings showing heliotrope rash are highly suggestive of\xa0dermatomyositis. Dermatomyositis involves CD 4+ cells,\xa0perimyseal infiltrates, and is associated with\xa0underlying malignancy.\\n\\nDermatomyositis is an idiopathic inflammatory myopathy characterized by proximal skeletal muscle weakness, muscle inflammation, and a variety of characteristic skin manifestations. The pattern of muscle involvement helps distinguish a myopathy from a cerebral, spinal cord, neuronal or neuromuscular junction cause. It is commonly associated with an underlying malignancy.\\n\\nClassic signs-\\n\\n• Heliotrope rash/ eyelid erythema- Violaceous erythema of the upper eyelids\\n• V sign-\xa0Confluent macular erythema over the lower anterior neck and upper anterior chest\\n• Shawl sign-\xa0Confluent macular erythema over the upper back and lateral aspect of arms\\n• Gottron’s papules-\xa0Erythematous papules and plaques over extensor surface of the metacarpophalangeal and interphalangeal joints\\n• Gottron\'s sign-\xa0Erythematous rash over the extensor surfaces of joints such as the knuckles, elbows, knees, and ankles.\\n• Mechanic’s hands-\xa0Rough, hyperkeratotic lines on the lateral and palmer areas of the fingers\\n• Scalp erythema and hair loss\\n• Facial rash- Confluent erythema in the malar distribution involving the cheeks, nasal bridge, and nasolabial folds\\n• Nail fold changes\\n• Flagellate erythema- Linear erythema on the upper back with pruritus\\n• Poikiloderma-\xa0Mottled pattern of hyperpigmented and hypopigmented macules with telangiectasias\\n• Cutaneous calcinosis- Accumulation of calcium deposits in the skin.\\n\\nLaboratory evaluation of patients with dermatomyositis will reveal\xa0elevated serum creatinine kinase\xa0and\xa0antinuclear antibodies. Electromyography will reveal features of myopathy. Histology will show\xa0perifascicular atrophy\xa0and immunohistochemical staining will reveal\xa0myxovirus resistance protein A, a sensitive and specific marker.\\n\\nTreatment of dermatomyositis is immunotherapy with\xa0high-dose glucocorticoids\xa0being the first line.\xa0Methotrexate\xa0can be started as the\xa0second line\xa0if the patient presents with severe weakness, other organ system involvement, or those with risk of steroid complications like patients with diabetes, osteoporosis, or postmenopausal women. Otherwise, second-line agents are only started in patients who do not show improvement after 2-4 months of treatment with prednisone or those who cannot be maintained on a low dose.\xa0Triple therapy\xa0with\xa0intravenous immunoglobulin\xa0or\xa0rituximab\xa0may be required.\\n\\nNote: Inclusion Body myositis is resistant to immunosuppressive medication\\n\\nMuscle biopsy findings in various inflammatory myositis are as follows-\\n\\nDermatomyositis\\nPolymyositis\\nInclusion body myositis\\n\\nPerimysial infiltrates\\nEndomysial infiltrates\\nEndomysial infiltrates\\n\\nPerivascular infiltrates and perifascicular atrophy.\\nPerivascular infiltrates\\nPerivascular infiltrates with amyloid inclusion bodies and rimmed vacuoles\\n\\nCD4+ cellular infiltrates\\nCD8+ cellular infiltrates\\nCD8+ cellular infiltrates","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/a003fe7dfd9411babc20.jpg"]},{"n":2,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A diagnosis of gouty arthritis is made after aspiration of the synovial fluid and polarised light microscopy shows negatively birefringent needle-shaped crystals as shown in the image given below. These crystals are suggestive of.","options":["Calcium apatite crystals","Monosodium urate crystals","Calcium Oxalate crystals","Calcium pyrophosphate crystals"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Monosodium urate crystals\\n\\nNegatively birefringent, needle-shaped crystals seen\xa0on polarised light microscopy in gouty\xa0arthritis is due to the deposition of\xa0monosodium urate crystals.\\n\\nThe\xa0 polarised light microscopic findings showing monosodium urate crystals is given below.,\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A- Calcium apatite crystals can accumulate in areas of tissue damage, hypercalcemic or hyperparathyroid states.\\n\\nOption C- Calcium Oxalate crystals are seen in chronic renal failure.\\n\\nOption D- Calcium pyrophosphate crystals are rhomboid-shaped and are seen in pseudogout, as shown in image below.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/a5b752d381bebcc9f6a7.png"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/c43e9f7e036656f26fe0.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/ba8738356aef92518e03.png"}]},{"n":3,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A patient presenting in mid-60s with vertical gaze palsy and early falls as well as the following picture on MRI, most likely has _______","options":["Parinaud’s syndrome","Corticobasal ganglionic degeneration","Weber’s syndrome","Progressive supranuclear palsy"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Progressive supranuclear palsy\\n\\nThe clinical features of this patient combined with the MRI findings are suggestive of\xa0progressive supranuclear palsy.\\n\\nProgressive supranuclear palsy is\xa0also known as Steele–Richardson–Olszewski syndrome. It is characterized by\xa0slow ocular Saccades, eyelid apraxia and restricted eye movements. Downward gaze is predominantly impaired. Early gait disturbance and falls are seen.\\n\\nThe given MRI images (midsagittal) show hummingbird sign. It occurs due to characteristic atrophy of the midbrain with relative preservation of the pons.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption a : Corticobasilar Ganglionic degeneration(CGBD) is an extremely rare neurodegenerative disease that is categorized under both Parkinsonian syndromes and frontal lobe dementias. It is characterized by parkinson\'s like motor symptoms and by deficits of movement and cognition, indicating focal brain pathology. Neuronal cell loss is ultimately responsible for the neurological symptoms. This patient’s gaze palsy makes PSP more likely.\\n\\nOption b : Parinaud\'s syndrome refers to supranuclear vertical gaze disorder. Here there is a loss of up-gaze and the downward gaze is usually preserved. Parinaud\'s syndrome/dorsal midbrain syndrome is caused by damage to the posterior commissure. Classically, signs of hydrocephalus are seen due to aqueductal stenosis. This patient has increased falls and Parkinson-like features non-responsive to L-dopa, which makes PSP more likely.\\n\\nOption c : Weber\'s syndrome is due to the involvement of the posterior cerebral artery. It affects the cranial nerve III, ventral midbrain, and corticospinal tract. Patients will have contralateral hemiplegia and contralateral paralysis of the lower part of the face. Ipsilateral oculomotor nerve palsy where the eyeball is deviated laterally because of the paralysis of the medial rectus muscle, and drooping of the upper lid (ptosis).","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/e3784c335be3de18e3bb.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/95a99b7a618adbddd62c.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/67eceb0c447275a28035.jpg"}]},{"n":4,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 30-year-old woman presents with amenorrhea, headache, vomiting, loss of libido, and galactorrhea from 3 months. On examination, her vitals are within range but her eye examination shows bitemporal hemianopia. Which of the following drug is preferred in this patient?","options":["Somatostatin","Thyroxine","Estradiol","Cabergoline"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Cabergoline\\n\\nThe given clinical vignette is suggestive of prolactinoma of the pituitary.\xa0Among the given options, cabergoline is the preferred drug for this patient.\\n\\nProlactinoma:\xa0Prolactin-secreting lactotroph adenomas are the most frequent type of hyperfunctioning pituitary adenomas. The symptoms include\\n\\n• Headache and vomiting\\n• Bitemporal hemianopia suggestive of a local mass effect\\n• Increased intracranial pressure due to a pituitary tumor\\n• In females-\xa0amenorrhea, galactorrhea, loss of libido, and infertility\\n• In males-\xa0impotence, loss of libido, and gynecomastia\\n\\nThe diagnosis of prolactinoma is most likely to be associated with a prolactin level >200 μg/L.\\n\\nTreatment is only required in symptomatic cases as microadenomas rarely progress to macroadenomas. The mainstay of treatment is medical management.\xa0Dopamine and dopamine agonists are used and they act as inhibitory signals to prolactin secretion in the pituitary.\\n\\nCabergoline is a\xa0long-acting\xa0dopamine receptor agonist with high D2\xa0receptor affinity and is the preferred drug for the treatment of hyperprolactinemia because of its greater efficacy and lower side effects.\xa0It effectively suppresses prolactin secretion for >14 days after a single oral dose.\xa0At higher doses, it is used in some patients with\xa0acromegaly, alone or along with somatostatin analogs.\\n\\nAnother drug used in prolactinoma is bromocriptine.\xa0It\xa0is shorter-acting when compared with cabergoline. The adverse effects of bromocriptine include hypotension and nausea.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":5,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 28-year-old female with a history of asthma, complaints of pain and numbness in both legs. Examination findings include palpable purpura and asymmetric mononeuritis multiplex. Peripheral blood smear findings show eosinophila. What could be the diagnosis?","options":["Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis","Polyarteritis nodosa","Churg Strauss syndrome","Giant cell arteritis"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Churg Strauss syndrome\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of Churg-Strauss syndrome (eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangitis).\\n\\nIt is a necrotizing vasculitis of the small-to-medium-sized vessels and in order to be diagnosed with Churg-Strauss, a patient should have evidence of asthma, peripheral blood and tissue eosinophilia (>1000 cells/μL), extravascular granuloma formation and clinical features consistent with vasculitis.\\n\\nCommon presenting symptoms of this condition are lower respiratory tract symptoms such as severe attacks of asthma, upper respiratory tract symptoms such as allergic rhinitis, sinusitis, and nasal polyps, mono neuritis multiplex (a type of peripheral neuropathy affecting at least two nerves) which presents with a complaint of pain, numbness, or weakness in the affected areas and skin lesions like palpable purpura and subcutaneous nodules.\xa0P-ANCA antibodies are present in Churg Strauss syndrome.\\n\\nGlucocorticoids show effective responses in these patients. Other drugs that have been tried are mepolizumab and rituximab. For fulminant multisystem disease, daily cyclophosphamide with prednisolone followed by azathioprine or methotrexate is used.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis presents with palpable purpura, arthralgia, neuropathy, and glomerulonephritis.\\n\\nOption B: Polyarteritis nodosa can also present with mononeuritis multiplex and skin lesions, but they do\xa0not\xa0have\xa0eosinophilia.\\n\\nOption D: Giant cell arteritis manifests with the cardinal symptom of headache localized to the temporal region.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":6,"subject":"Medicine","text":"The lymph node biopsy of a 49-year-old woman with painless lymphadenopathy revealed the following finding. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Lymphocyte-depleted Hodgkin\'s disease","Lymphocyte-rich Hodgkin\'s disease","Nodular Sclerosis classical Hodgkin\'s disease","None of the above"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Nodular Sclerosis classical Hodgkin\'s disease\\n\\nThe histopathological finding is of lacunar Reed-Sternberg (RS cell) cell, which is seen in Nodular Sclerosis classical Hodgkin\'s disease.\\n\\nReed-Sternberg cell, lacunar variant\xa0has a folded or multilobated nucleus and lies within an open space, which is an artifact created by disruption of the cytoplasm during tissue sectioning.\\n\\nHistological subtypes of Classic Hodgkin\'s disease\\n\\nSubtype\\nHistopathology\\nClinical correlation\\n\\nNodular Sclerosis\\n\\n• Lacunar RS cell + necrosis +inflammatory infiltrate with eosinophils, macrophages, and plasma cells\\n• Fibrous bands dividing cellular areas into nodules\\n\\n• Mediastinal lymphadenopathy and superior vena cava syndrome\\n• Excellent prognosis\\n\\nMixed cellularity\\nDiagnostic RS cells with rich inflammatory infiltrate\\n\\n• Biphasic: Children and elderly\\n• B symptoms\\n• Associated with EBV (and HIV),\\n• Very good prognosis\\n\\nLymphocyte-rich\\nBackground infiltrate rich\\nin T lymphocytes\\n\\nLymphocyte- depleted\\nReticular variant, paucity of background reactive cells\\n\\n• Associated with HIV (and EBV)\\n• Poor prognosis\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption a : Lymphocyte-depleted Hodgkin\'s disease, has Reticular variant of RS cell. It is associated with HIV and EBV. It has poor prognosis.\\n\\nOption b :\xa0Lymphocyte-rich Hodgkin\'s disease,\xa0is a rare subtype of Hodgkin lymphoma (HL) characterized by a small number of Hodgkin/Reed-Sternberg (HRS) cells and a background of small lymphocytes","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/1eaf0d1a3dc8958d1e41.png"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/4b661e0fc54c9bd6ae81.png"}]},{"n":7,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 30-year-old woman presents with a 3-month history of amenorrhea, headache, vomiting, loss of libido, and galactorrhea. On examination, her vital signs are within normal limits, but her eye examination reveals bitemporal hemianopia. Which of the following drugs is preferred for this patient?","options":["Thyroxine","Estradiol","Cabergoline","Somatostatin"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cabergoline\\n\\nThe given clinical vignette is suggestive of prolactinoma of the pituitary.\xa0Among the given options, cabergoline is the preferred drug for this patient.\\n\\nProlactinoma:\xa0Prolactin-secreting lactotroph adenomas are the most frequent type of hyperfunctioning pituitary adenomas. The symptoms include\\n\\n• Headache and vomiting\\n• Bitemporal hemianopia suggestive of a local mass effect\\n• Increased intracranial pressure due to a pituitary tumor\\n• In females-\xa0amenorrhea, galactorrhea, loss of libido, and infertility\\n• In males-\xa0impotence, loss of libido, and gynecomastia\\n\\nThe diagnosis of prolactinoma is most likely to be associated with a prolactin level >200 μg/L.\\n\\nTreatment is only required in symptomatic cases as microadenomas rarely progress to macroadenomas. The mainstay of treatment is medical management.\xa0Dopamine and dopamine agonists are used and they act as inhibitory signals to prolactin secretion in the pituitary.\\n\\nCabergoline is a\xa0long-acting\xa0dopamine receptor agonist with high D2\xa0receptor affinity and is the preferred drug for the treatment of hyperprolactinemia because of its greater efficacy and lower side effects.\xa0It effectively suppresses prolactin secretion for >14 days after a single oral dose.\xa0At higher doses, it is used in some patients with\xa0acromegaly, alone or along with somatostatin analogs.\\n\\nAnother drug used in prolactinoma is bromocriptine.\xa0It\xa0is shorter-acting when compared with cabergoline. The adverse effects of bromocriptine include hypotension and nausea.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Thyroxine: Used for hypothyroidism, not indicated here unless there is concurrent hypothyroidism.\\n\\nOption B: Estradiol: Could address hypogonadism due to elevated prolactin but does not treat the underlying prolactinoma.\\n\\nOption D: Somatostatin: Used primarily for growth hormone-secreting tumors, not effective for prolactinomas.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":8,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 45-year-old patient presents with reduced urine output and is diagnosed with CKD Stage 5, requiring dialysis. During the procedure, the patient experiences an episode of hypotension. Which of the following cannot prevent dialysis-associated hypotension?\\n\\nCKD-Chronic Kidney Disease","options":["Maintaining fluid rate of >13 mL/kg/hour.","Careful dry weight evaluation","Avoiding heavy meals","Cooling of the dialysate"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Maintaining fluid rate of >13 mL/kg/hour.\\n\\nMaintaining the fluid rate at > 13 mL/kg/hour will not\xa0prevent hypotension during dialysis.\\n\\nThe fluid rate has to be maintained at less than 13mL/kg/hour to prevent hypotension.\\n\\nHypotension is the most common acute complication of hemodialysis. The mechanism is not fully understood and occurs due to multiple factors. However, the following precautions have been shown to frequently prevent this complication:\\n\\n• Careful calculation of the dry weight (Option B) and adjustment of the filtration rate accordingly.\\n• Ultrafiltration modeling such that more fluid is removed during the beginning than at the end of the procedure.\\n• Avoiding rapid fluid removal and maintaining the rate < 13 mL/kg/hour.\\n• Avoiding heavy meals during dialysis (Option C).\\n• Cooling of the dialysate during the dialysis (Option D)","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":9,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 26-year-old male presents to the OPD with mild fever since 12 days, non-productive cough, headache, and neck rigidity. The patient also complained of multiple joint pains. A chest x-ray was done which shows interstitial infiltration. Which is the most likely causative organism for this condition?","options":["Staphylococcus aureus","Pneumococcus","Legionella","Mycoplasma"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Mycoplasma\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario with chest X-ray showing interstitial infiltration along with patchy consolidation at hilum and fanning to the periphery is suggestive of atypical pneumonia caused by Mycoplasma.\\n\\nMycoplasma is the most common bacteria causing atypical pneumonia.\xa0Clinically it has an insidious onset, non-productive cough, and milder fever compared to typical pneumonia. URTI (tracheobronchitis/ pharyngitis)\xa0is a common pulmonary manifestation.\\n\\nIt is often associated with\xa0extrapulmonary manifestations. Extrapulmonary manifestations include neurologic manifestations like encephalitis or aseptic meningitis (as seen in this patient), cold agglutinin disease (IgM mediated AIHA), Erythema multiforme or SJS, arthralgia with adenopathy, Guillain barre syndrome, myocarditis, pericarditis, encephalitis, and bullous myringitis.\\n\\nInvestigations include PCR of respiratory secretions and chest X-rays. Azithromycin\xa0is the\xa0drug of choice\xa0for management.\xa0Doxycycline\xa0is the\xa0second-best drug\xa0for the management of atypical pneumonia.\\n\\nThe chest X-ray showing interstitial infiltration along with patchy consolidation at hilum and fanning to the periphery is shown in the image below.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption a : Pneumococci are commensals of the upper respiratory tract and can cause pneumonia, sinusitis, otitis, bronchitis, bacteremia, meningitis, peritonitis, and other infectious processes.The onset of pneumococcal pneumonia is usually sudden, with fever, chills, and sharp pleural pain. The sputum is similar to the alveolar exudate, being characteristically bloody or rusty colored. Disseminated infection and bacteremia can present as meningitis, endocarditis, and septic arthritis.\\n\\nOption c : Legionella pneumophila is a fastidious, aerobic gram-negative coccobacillus. It can be grown on a buffered charcoal yeast extract (BCYE) agar with L-cysteine and antibiotic supplements. Diagnosis is by the identification of legionella antigens in urine by latex agglutination or Enzyme Linked Immunosorbent Assay (ELISA). Treatment is by newer macrolides, ciprofloxacin or tetracycline. Aminoglycoside and beta lactam antibiotics are ineffective.\\n\\nOption d : \xa0Staphylococcus aureus is a gram-positive coccus arranged in grape-like clusters. It is coagulase and catalase positive. It\xa0causes bacterial pneumonia with features similar to pneumococcal pneumonia. The differentiating feature is that chest X-ray in Staph. aureus infection shows pneumatoceles (cavitation).\\n\\nThe X-ray shows pneumatoceles (air-filled cysts within lung parenchyma).","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/5a59e0950998d313b795.png"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/2d0eb140e5afa42c9500.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/273eaabdbe7f9f22f066.jpg"}]},{"n":10,"subject":"Medicine","text":"Identify the tachycardia depicted in the given ECG.","options":["Ventricular fibrillation","Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter","Ventricular tachycardia"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Atrial flutter\\n\\nThe given ECG is suggestive of atrial flutter.\\n\\nThe given ECG shows narrow QRS complexes, hence it is supraventricular tachycardia. The RR intervals are regular and the normal P waves are replaced by sawtooth-shaped waves\xa0(red arrows), known as flutter waves. Hence, it is most likely to be atrial flutter.\\n\\nIn atrial flutter, the ventricular rate is determined by the AV conduction ratio. The commonest AV ratio is 2:1. As the atrial rate is around 300 bpm, AV conduction ratio of 2:1 results in a ventricular rate of ~150 bpm.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption a : Atrial fibrillation rhythm is irregularly irregular. P waves are absent, these irregular waves are known as fibrillation waves, characteristic of atrial fibrillation, as shown in the ECG below.\\n\\nOption c : Ventricular tachycardia ecg changes include wide QRS complexes, absence of P waves, and ventriculoatrial dissociation. In monomorphic VT, all QRS complexes look the same. Polymorphic VT has QRS complexes varying in shape or direction in the same lead.\\n\\nThe ecg of ventricular tachycardia is given below.\\n\\nOption d : Ventricular fibrillation\xa0shows a completely disorganized rhythm with no identifiable waves on ECG, as shown in the image below.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/454244aa1ff61e3c0522.png"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/986978933e1b5cc38c41.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/2c080337bbe323c83bc4.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/3a6e78a1e8d0bdd4a81b.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/f690ce118f6e100d26f7.jpg"}]},{"n":11,"subject":"Medicine","text":"An IT professional diagnosed with vitamin D deficiency during a routine check-up began taking 60,000 IU vitamin D sachets daily. Two weeks later, he presented with severe colicky pain in the renal angle. His vitamin D levels are elevated (170 ng/mL). Which medication would be helpful in treating this condition?","options":["Calcitonin","Aspirin","Trientine","Zinc"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Calcitonin\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario depicts vitamin D toxicity and\xa0calcitonin can be used in the treatment of this condition.\\n\\nVitamin D toxicity occurs due to excessive intake of vitamin D supplements, usually in amounts of >40,000–100,000 IU/day. The symptoms are mainly due to hypercalcemia.\xa0Laboratory investigations will show elevated serum vitamin D levels (>100 ng/mL) and calcium.\\n\\nIn such patients, vitamin D supplements should be discontinued immediately, along with restricted exposure to the sun and avoiding calcium supplements. Since vitamin D is stored in the body for a long time, the symptoms may persist for some days even after stopping the intake.\\n\\nCalcitonin and bisphosphonates are used in cases of severe toxicity causing hypercalcemia. Some patients might require glucocorticoids, which lower plasma calcium levels by reducing calcium absorption from the intestines and increasing its urinary excretion.\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption B- Aspirin is used in the management of niacin-induced\xa0flushing.\\n\\nOptions C and D- Trientine and zinc are copper chelators. They are mainly used in the treatment of Wilson\'s disease.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":12,"subject":"Medicine","text":"Examination of the oral cavity of a patient with acute myeloid leukemia (AML) revealed the findings shown in the picture below. Which of the following is the most likely subtype of AML in this patient?","options":["M3","M1","M5","M7"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. M5\\n\\nThe most likely subtype of Acute Myeloid Leukemia in this patient is AML-M5.\\n\\nThe picture shows swollen and spongy gums in a patient with AML.\xa0Gum infiltration is characteristic of acute monocytic leukemia (AML-M5). Infiltration of the gingivae, skin, soft tissues or meninges with leukemic blasts is characteristic of the monocytic subtypes and those with 11q23 chromosomal abnormalities. If M5 is not given in options, the next best answer would be acute myelomonocytic leukemia (AML-M4).\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption a : AML-M7 is associated with downs syndrome and bone marrow fibrosis.\\n\\nOption c : AML-M3 is associated with DIC (disseminated intravascular coagulation).\\n\\nOption d : AML-M1 is associated AML without maturation.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/94781d8fd58e734243bb.jpg"]},{"n":13,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A woman presents to you with fever, arthralgia, ulcers, fatigue for the past six months, and new-onset hematuria. Urine examination reveals RBC casts and proteinuria. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Acute interstitial nephritis","Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis","Lupus nephritis","IgA nephropathy"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Lupus nephritis\\n\\nThe above clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of lupus nephritis.\\n\\nLupus nephritis is a common complication of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). It results from immune complex deposition containing DNA and anti-DNA.\xa0Clinical features include proteinuria, hematuria, and hypertension. The disease is divided into 6 classes based on the severity. Patients with class V disease are at an increased risk of thrombotic complications. Lupus nephritis can result in end-stage disease warranting dialysis or transplantation.\\n\\nSLE is an autoimmune condition more commonly seen in women. Antinuclear antibodies, anti-dsDNA, and anti-Sm are the autoantibodies implicated. Symptoms include maculopapular butterfly rash, photosensitivity, oral or nasal ulcers, fever, and intermittent polyarthritis. Pleuritis and pericarditis can also be associated. Systemic glucocorticoids, cyclophosphamide, or mycophenolate mofetil are used for management. Due to the possible teratogenic effects of these drugs, hydroxychloroquine is the mainstay in pregnancy.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A - Acute interstitial nephritis is a type of acute kidney injury. It is marked by oliguria and raised creatinine. Various causes of interstitial injury include the use of non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), pyelonephritis, leukemia, and sepsis. White blood cell (WBC)\xa0casts and granular casts may be seen.\\n\\nOption B - Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis usually affects male children between the age of 2-14. Skin or pharyngeal infection with M types of the organism is implicated. Symptoms include hematuria, pyuria, and hypertension. Flank pain and headache are also associated symptoms. Red blood cell (RBC) casts are seen in the urine.\\n\\nOption D - IgA nephropathy arises due to mesangial IgA deposition. It is characterized by recurrent macroscopic hematuria after an upper respiratory infection associated with proteinuria. Mesangial proliferation is a distinctive feature. Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors and steroids can be used for management.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":14,"subject":"Medicine","text":"In Addison’s disease, the most commonly implemented test for clinical diagnosis is","options":["Determination of Plasma ACTH levels","Serum Na+, K+, Renin","ACTH Stimulation Test","Serum Na+, K+, Saline Suppression Test"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. ACTH Stimulation Test\\n\\nThe commonly implemented method of diagnosis of Addissons disease (adrenal insufficiency) is the ACTH Stimulation Test.\\n\\nAdrenal insufficiency is the decreased production of adrenocortical hormones and is classified as primary or secondary. Evaluation of adrenal insufficiency begins with clinical suspicion based on symptoms such as weight loss, fatigue, postural hypotension, hyperpigmentation, and hyponatremia.\\n\\nDiagnosis is confirmed with a cosyntropin stimulation test, which measures the adrenal glands response to synthetic ACTH (cosyntropin). A baseline cortisol level is obtained, followed by cosyntropin administration, with cortisol levels measured again after 30-60 minutes. In adrenal insufficiency, cortisol levels remain low despite stimulation.\\n\\nDifferentiation between primary and secondary causes involves measuring plasma ACTH levels. High ACTH levels indicate primary adrenal insufficiency, warranting adrenal autoantibody tests and further imaging like chest X-ray or adrenal CT to determine the cause. Low or normal ACTH levels indicate secondary adrenal insufficiency, necessitating an MRI of the pituitary to identify possible lesions or other underlying causes (in patients not taking corticosteroids).","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/e20e223822d3712defef.jpg"}]},{"n":15,"subject":"Medicine","text":"In asterixis, which of the following would commonly be seen?","options":["Thyrotoxicosis","Anxiety neurosis","Hypoxia","Hypercarbia"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Hypercarbia\\n\\nIn asterixis, the condition commonly seen is hypercarbia. Asterixis is frequently observed in patients with hypercarbia, especially in the setting of respiratory failure or COPD, due to its effects on the central nervous system. Therefore, hypercarbia is the most appropriate option among the choices provided.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nHypoxia refers to a condition in which there is a deficiency of oxygen reaching the tissues. While hypoxia can lead to various neurological manifestations, asterixis is not typically associated with hypoxia. Asterixis is more commonly observed in conditions affecting the central nervous system or metabolic disturbances.\\n\\nThyrotoxicosis is a condition characterized by excessive thyroid hormone levels in the bloodstream, often due to an overactive thyroid gland (hyperthyroidism). Asterixis is not a characteristic feature of thyrotoxicosis. Instead, thyrotoxicosis may present with symptoms such as palpitations, heat intolerance, weight loss, and tremors.\\n\\nAnxiety neurosis, or anxiety disorder, refers to a group of mental health disorders characterized by excessive worry, fear, and nervousness. Asterixis is not typically associated with anxiety neurosis. While anxiety disorders can manifest with various physical symptoms, asterixis is more commonly observed in neurological or metabolic conditions.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":16,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 59-year-old woman presented to the emergency room within two hours of sudden onset of weakness of the right side. On examination, she had global aphasia, right facial droop, and right hemiplegia. CT angiography showed a left middle cerebral artery occlusion. Which of the following is the next best management?","options":["Nothing, since CT was normal","Start on aspirin + clopidogrel","Intravenous thrombolysis","Advice BP control"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Intravenous thrombolysis\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario with CT angiography showing a left middle cerebral artery occlusion is suggestive of ischemic stroke and the next best step of management is intravenous thrombolysis.\\n\\nTreatment of acute ischemic stroke:\\n\\nInitial treatment: Check for the patient\'s pulse rate, blood pressure, temperature, and glucose levels. Treat hyperglycemia, hypoglycemia, or fever if present. Maintain the patient\'s airway, breathing, and circulation.\\n\\nBlood pressure should be reduced acutely if :\\n\\n• Blood pressure > 220/120 mmHg.\\n• There is malignant hypertension.\\n• There is concomitant myocardial ischemia.\\n• Blood pressure is > 185/110 mmHg and the patient is being planned for intravenous thrombolysis.\\n\\nSerum glucose should be maintained between 180mg/dL and 60mg/dL. Ischemia can result in edema which peaks on the second or third day and can lead to a mass effect for around 10 days. Therefore, intravenous mannitol should be started and water restriction should be advised for the patients.\\n\\nSpecific measures:\\n\\nIntravenous thrombolysis with recombinant tissue plasminogen activator (rTPA), alteplase should be immediately administered to patients with ischemic stroke who arrive in the window period (within 4.5 hours of symptom onset).\\n\\nEndovascular mechanical thrombectomy is done in patients who do not respond to thrombolytic therapy or in whom thrombolysis is contraindicated.\\n\\nOral aspirin with an initial dose of 325 mg should be started within 24-48 hours of acute ischemic stroke. It is also continued as an adjunct therapy after intravenous thrombolysis.\\n\\nRoutine use of heparin and other oral anticoagulants is not recommended in patients with ischemic stroke because they can increase the risk of brain and systemic hemorrhage. They can be used if the patient is bedridden, in order to prevent deep vein thrombosis. (Option B)","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":17,"subject":"Medicine","text":"Pulsus bisferiens is seen in all of the following conditions except ______\\n\\nAS-Aortic stenosis\\n\\nAR-Aortic regurgitation","options":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Advanced aortic regurgitation","Left ventricular dysfunction","AS with AR"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Left ventricular dysfunction\\n\\nPulsus bisferiens is seen in all the given conditions except, left ventricular dysfunction.\\n\\nPulsus bisferiens is a bifid pulse with two systolic peaks.\xa0It is seen in the following conditions :\\n\\n• Advanced aortic regurgitation (Option a).\\n• Aortic stenosis (AS) with Aortic regurgitation (Option b).\\n• Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (Option d).\\n\\nLeft ventricular dysfunction has pulsus alternans.\\n\\nThe various varieties of arterial pulsus and their waveforms have been represented below:","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a57f5d53efcb888f323e.jpg"}]},{"n":18,"subject":"Medicine","text":"Which of the following is not characteristic of central apnea?","options":["Transient cessation of neural drive to all respiratory muscles","Is due to decreased sensitivity to pCO2","Associated with cheyne stokes breathing","Opioids are a risk factor"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Is due to decreased sensitivity to pCO2\\n\\nAll the given statements are true about central sleep apnea except, it is due to decreased sensitivity to pCO2. It is due to increased sensitivity to pCO2.\\n\\nAn increase in pCO2 in normal individuals leads to reflex hyperventilation to restore the pCO2 levels to normal. But in CSA, due to increased sensitivity to pCO2\xa0at the central respiratory center, even very mild pCO2 can cause abnormally increased hyperventilation which leads to washing off of pCO2 below normal levels, which in turn leads to apnea.\\n\\nThus changes in pCO2 levels during sleep lead to an unstable breathing pattern that manifests as hyperventilation alternating with apnea. Therefore, a Crescendo-decrescendo breathing pattern known as Cheyne-Stokes respiration is seen.\\n\\nRisk factors for CSA include opioid medications\xa0and hypoxia (e.g., breathing at high altitude). In some individuals, CPAP, particularly at high pressures\xa0seems to induce central apnea; this condition is referred to as complex sleep apnea.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption a : CSA is associated with transient cessation of neural drive to all respiratory muscles.\\n\\nOption b : CSA is associated with a Crescendo-decrescendo breathing pattern known as Cheyne-Stokes respiration.\\n\\nOption d : Opiod medication is a risk factor for CSA.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":19,"subject":"Medicine","text":"Which of the following is associated with steatorrhea?","options":["Short bowel syndrome (SBS)","Primary lactase deficiency","Pernicious anemia","Wilson\'s disease"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Short bowel syndrome (SBS)\\n\\nShort bowel syndrome is associated with steatorrhoea which is defined as excess fat in stools (>7g/day) due to poor absorption of lipids.\\n\\nShort bowel syndrome refers to intractable diarrhea with malabsorption of nutrients after intestinal resection or intestinal bypass surgery leading to malnutrition. The features of short bowel syndrome usually appear when the length is less than 200 cm of the small bowel, but the length and nature of the remaining bowel are also important. The common causes of short bowel syndrome include acute mesenteric ischemia, Crohn’s disease, trauma and radiation enteritis in adults, and bowel atresia and midgut volvulus in children\\n\\nThe clinical features include:\\n\\n• Intractable diarrhea (due to hypergastrinemia and gastric hypersecretion, or unabsorbed bile salts in the colon).\\n• Steatorrhea (due to the depletion of the bile salt pool), gallstones (due to interruption of the enterohepatic cycle of bile salts).\\n• Renal stones (due to increased absorption of oxalate in the colon).\\n• Peptic ulcer (due to hypergastrinemia and gastric hypersecretion).\\n• Fat-soluble (vitamin A, D, E, and K) and B12 vitamin deficiencies.\\n\\nInitial treatment priorities include repletion of fluid and electrolytes lost in severe diarrhea by initiating total parenteral nutrition (TPN). Home parenteral nutrition is the mainstay of the treatment. Long-term management involves:\\n\\n• Administration of oral cholestyramine to bind with bile salts\\n• Dietary fat restriction\\n• Parenteral vitamin and mineral supplements\\n• Proton pump inhibitors to lower gastric pH\\n• Octreotide to reduce the volume of GI secretions\\n• Antimotility agents such as loperamide hydrochloride to delay small-intestinal transit\\n• Hormonal therapy such as growth hormone, glutamine, and GLP-2 (glucagon-like peptide-2) analog (teduglutide) are used to promote intestinal adaptation.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Primary lactase deficiency leads to defective absorption of lactose. It does not affect lipid absorption.\\n\\nOption C: Pernicious anemia is due to an absence of gastric parietal cell-intrinsic factor which is necessary for the absorption of vitamin B12\\n\\nOption D: Wilsons disease is associated with increased absorption of copper and is not a part of malabsorption syndrome.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":20,"subject":"Medicine","text":"Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) score is decreased in all except ___________.","options":["Chronic Myeloid Leukemia","Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria","Sickle Cell Anemia","Polycythemia Vera"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Polycythemia Vera\\n\\nLeukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) score is decreased in all the given conditions except, polycythemia vera.\\n\\nLAP score is characteristically increased in\xa0Leukemoid reaction and Polycythemia Vera.\\n\\nNote: Leukemoid reaction: Extreme elevations in WBC counts >50,000/cu.mm due to reactive causes outside the marrow. eg. severe infection, trauma, bone marrow infiltration, Down syndrome.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nLAP score is decreased in chronic myeloid leukemia (Option A), paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria (Option B) and sickle cell anemia (Option C).","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":21,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 45-year-old woman with a history of hypertension presented to the emergency room with loss of consciousness, chest pain, and diaphoresis. On examination, she was grossly unstable and the bilateral pulses were unequal. ECG showed nonspecific ST-T changes. What is the next best investigation?","options":["X-ray","Cardiac enzymes","MRI","Transesophageal echocardiography"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Transesophageal echocardiography\\n\\nThe given clinical presentation with acute onset chest pain, loss of consciousness and diaphoresis with bilaterally unequal pulses and ECG showing nonspecific ST-T changes is suggestive of aortic dissection, and as the patient is\xa0hemodynamically unstable, the next best investigation is transesophageal ECHO.\\n\\nAortic dissection occurs when a defect or flap occurs in the intima of the aorta. Predisposing factors include mainly age, hypertension, Marfan syndrome, and pregnancy. The most typical presentation of aortic dissection is a tearing interscapular pain. The dissection may also result in aortic wall rupture into the pericardium (cardiac tamponade) or mediastinum (left haemothorax).\\n\\nContrast-enhanced CT(CECT) is currently the most preferred imaging modality for aortic dissection. A double-lumen aorta is seen on CECT. Transesophageal echocardiography is preferred for patients who are unstable for transportation.\\n\\nAcute aortic dissection can be classified by the original DeBakey system, based on the location of the proximal tear and distal extent of the dissection flap, or the Stanford system, which uses the location of the proximal tear alone.\\n\\nThe classification and management of aortic dissection is explained in the images given below.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/1adc7c1e2c735d17cd5f.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/ef0c6d30109ecb73d8c7.jpg"}]},{"n":22,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 25 year old female came with complaints of fatigue and neck swelling since past 4 months. She delivered a baby 4 months ago and is currently breastfeeding her child. Lab investigations reveal elevated serum TSH with normal T3,T4 levels. Physical examination shows slight enlargement of thyroid gland but everything else is normal. All the following are true regarding the given condition except?","options":["It is associated with Type 1 diabetes","Thyroxine replacement therapy is given life long","It is associated with transient production of anti thyroid antibodies","Follow up with TSH is done after 2 month"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Thyroxine replacement therapy is given life long\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario of a postpartum female with painless neck swelling points towards diagnosis of Postpartum thyroiditis and lifelong thyroxine replacement therapy is NOT necessary in management of this condition. The next step in management is to reassure her and do a follow-up TSH 2 months later.\\n\\nPostpartum thyroiditis is a subset of silent thyroiditis. It is specifically used to describe the constellation of symptoms of silent thyroiditis in a postpartum patient 3-6 months after birth. It occurs due to the transient production of anti-thyroid antibodies during periods of stress (Option C). The condition is observed in up to 5% of women and it is 3 times more common in women with type 1 diabetes mellitus. (Option A)\\n\\nTypically, patients experience a brief phase of thyrotoxicosis lasting 2-4 weeks, followed by hypothyroidism for 4-12 weeks, and then clinical resolution. Often, only one phase is apparent. Subacute thyroiditis often has a normal ESR value and TPO antibodies.\\n\\nSevere thyrotoxic symptoms can be managed with a brief course of propranolol, a 20–40 mg dose administered three or four times daily.\\n\\nThyroxine replacement may be needed for treating the hypothyroid phase but should be withdrawn after 6–9 months, to enable patient recovery. This patient with mild symptoms of hypothyroidism should be managed expectantly. Patients should be followed up every 2 months with TSH measures to check for symptom resolution and analyze the need for hormone supplementation. (Option D)","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":23,"subject":"Medicine","text":"An HIV-positive patient presents with CD4 count < 50/μL complaints of fever, weight loss and night sweats. Which of the following investigations would you order to confirm an atypical mycobacterial infection?","options":["Cartridge based nucleic acid amiplification test","Blood culture","Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid cytology","Interferon gamma release assay"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Blood culture\\n\\nInfection with\xa0 Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) is confirmed by blood culture. MAC is the atypical Mycobacterium most commonly associated with human disease. MAC is primarily a pulmonary pathogen that affects immune-compromised individuals.\\n\\nMAC is an atypical mycobacterial infection commonly seen in HIV-infected patients most commonly in those with CD4 count < 50/μL, presenting with fever, weight loss and night sweats. Chest X-ray may show bilateral lower lobe infiltrates, or alveolar infiltrates and mediastinal adenopathy. Diagnosis is made by blood culture or two positive sputum cultures.\\n\\nAs per NACO guidelines, routine prophylaxis is not recommended in India. Therapy for MAC infection is with Clarithromycin or Azithromycin + Ethambutol.\\n\\nOther Options:\\n\\nCBNAAT, IFN-\uD835\uDEC4 assay and BAL may all used to diagnose M. tuberculosis but are not used in the lab diagnosis of MAC. On the other hand, blood culture is not used to diagnose TB.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":24,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A patient with a short PR interval and delta wave on ECG presents to the emergency department with atrial fibrillation and rapid ventricular rate. He is hemodynamically stable. All of the following drugs can be used in the management except ________","options":["Ibutilide","Procainamide","Amiodarone","Verapamil"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Verapamil\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of wolf parkinson syndrome and all the following drugs can be used in the management of WPW syndrome with Atrial fibrillation except Verapamil.\\n\\nRate-slowing drugs such as non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (eg, verapamil, diltiazem), beta-blockers and digoxin are contraindicated in these patients. They increase the refractoriness of the AV node with minimal or no effect on the accessory pathway. This can lead to faster ventricular rates, increasing the risk of ventricular fibrillation.\\n\\nThe drugs of choice are antiarrhythmic drugs, procainamide (Class 1a), ibutilide (class III),\xa0or amiodarone\xa0that prolong the refractory period of the accessory pathway.\\n\\nIf the patient is hemodynamically unstable, the treatment of choice\xa0is direct-current cardioversion.\\n\\nThe ECG below shows a short PR interval (<120 ms) and delta waves in WPW syndrome.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption a : Amiodarone is a class III anti-arrythmic drug used in management of WPW syndrome with atrial fibrillation.\\n\\nOption b : Ibutilide is class III anti-arrythmic drug used in management of WPW syndrome with atrial fibrillation.\\n\\nOption c : Procainamide is class Ia anti-arrythmic drug used in management of WPW syndrome with atrial fibrillation.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/1676894cd9894c8e06da.jpg"}]},{"n":25,"subject":"Medicine","text":"Mr.Rajesh comes with complaints of non-pulsatile headache of tightening quality on both sides of his forehead. He describes it as wearing a tight cap. He has no nausea, photophobia, or phonophobia. All of the following drugs can be used in the treatment of this condition except","options":["Sumatriptan","Acetaminophen","Aspirin","Verapamil"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Sumatriptan\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of tension type headache and sumatriptan is not useful in treatment of this condition.\\n\\nTension-type headache(TTH) is commonly used to describe a chronic head-pain syndrome characterized by bilateral tight, band-like discomfort. The pain typically builds slowly, fluctuates in severity, and may persist more or less continuously for many days.\\n\\nThe headache may be episodic or chronic (present >15 days per month). A useful clinical approach is to diagnose TTH in patients whose headaches are complete without accompanying features such as nausea, vomiting, photophobia, phonophobia, osmophobia, throbbing, and aggravation with movement.\\n\\nTreatment of TTH includes simple analgesics such as NSAIDs and paracetamol. Amitriptyline has been found to be useful for chronic TTH. Triptans are not useful in the treatment of this condition.\\n\\nTypes of headache and there treatment is given in the below table.\\n\\nTension-type headache\\nMigraine\\nCluster headache\\n\\nAcute treatment\\n\\nAspirin\\n\\nNSAIDs\\n\\nAcetaminophen\\n\\nNSAIDs\\n\\nTriptans\\n\\nDihydroergotamine\\n\\n100% oxygen\\n\\nSumatriptan\\n\\nPreventive\\nAmitriptyline\\n\\n• Lifestyle changes(eg, sleep,exercise, diet)\\n• β-blockers\\n• Calcium channel blockers\\n• Amitriptyline, topiramate, valproate\\n• CGRP antagonists - erenumab, fremanezumab, galcanezumab, Eptinezumab\\n\\nVerapamil\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption a : Aspirin is used in treatment of tension type headache.\\n\\nOption c : Acetaminophen is\xa0used in treatment of tension type headache.\\n\\nOption d : Verapamil is a CCB (Calcium channel blocker), used in prevention of cluster headache.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":26,"subject":"Medicine","text":"Which of the following disease pathogenesis primarily involves the marked structure?","options":["Huntington chorea","Depression","Parkinson\'s disease","Alzheimers"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Parkinson\'s disease\\n\\nThe disease pathogenesis primarily involving the marked structure is Parkinson\'s diease. The structure marked in this transverse section of the brain is substantia nigra.\\n\\nSubstantia nigra is pigmented due to the presence of neuromelanin granules, which help in the\xa0synthesis of dopamine.\xa0In Parkinson\'s disease,\xa0 there is absence/reduced pigmentation of the substantia nigra.\\n\\nBradykinesia, rigidity and resting tremor are classic features of Parkinson\'s disease. Because of motor disability, it is also referred to as paralysis agitans.\\n\\nThe image below is the cross section at the level of superior colliculus showing absent or reduced pigmentation of the substantia nigra.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption b : Depression is often a comorbid finding with Parkinson\'s disease, but is not due to damage to the substantia nigra.\\n\\nOption c : Alzheimer’s disease is the most common cause of dementia. It is due to atrophy of the entorhinal cortex, located in the medial temporal lobe.\\n\\nOption d : Huntington’s chorea is an autosomal dominant disorder of movement and cognition primarily involving the striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen).","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/d990dd5f78c94ac1cdc7.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/85cdba5c87b935031732.jpg"}]},{"n":27,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 35-year-old male patient reports decreased sensation to pain in his fingertips over the past two months, particularly when exposed to cold weather. On examination, his toes exhibit the findings shown in the image below, and his fingertips appear waxy and mottled. All of the following measures are correct regarding the management of this condition, except:","options":["Analgesics are often required during rewarming.","Rapid rewarming can be accomplished by immersing the foot in a warm water bath","Rubbing/friction can be used to hasten up the process of rewarming","Tetanus prophylaxis must be given as there is increased risk of tetanus in frost bite"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Rubbing/friction can be used to hasten up the process of rewarming\\n\\nRewarming by rubbing/ friction is not advised in the case of a frostbite.\\n\\nIn frostbite, tissue damage results from\xa0severe\xa0environmental\xa0cold exposure or direct contact with a very cold object. Tissue\xa0injury results from both freezing and vascular stasis. It usually\xa0affects\xa0the distal aspects of the\xa0extremities\xa0or exposed parts of\xa0the face, such as the ears, nose, chin, and cheeks.\\n\\nClinical features include decreased sensation (especially for pain, and temperature sensations) in the acral tips and extremities. The affected area appears waxy, mottled, or violaceous. Patients may have a wood-like sensation in the affected area.\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\n• Initial\xa0treatment\xa0is by rapid\xa0rewarming\xa0(or thawing), which is accomplished by immersion of the affected part in a warm water bath at temperatures of 37\xb0–40\xb0C (99\xb0–104\xb0F) for 30–60 min (Option B). Rewarming by exercise, rubbing, or friction is not advised.\\n• Analgesics such as ibuprofen are often required during rewarming(Option A).\\n• Antithrombotics, thrombolytics and vasodilators such as prostacyclin can be given to improve circulation after rewarming.\\n• Frost bites are associated with an increased risk of infections and tetanus. Therefore, the injured area should\xa0be cleaned with soap or antiseptic, and sterile dressings should be\xa0applied. Tetanus prophylaxis must be given (Option D), but empirical antibiotics are not recommended.\\n• The patient must be monitored for the development of compartment syndrome.\\n• Elective late\xa0amputation after imaging may be required in severe cases.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/79e9625a57b0ee3d9fbf.jpg"]},{"n":28,"subject":"Medicine","text":"The arterial blood gas analysis of a 47-year-old diabetic patient revealed the following values: pH- 7.2 and HCO3- 15 mEq/L. Which of the following statements is false regarding renal compensation in this patient?","options":["Bicarbonate reabsorption is decreased","Urinary acidity is increased","Hydrogen ion secretion is increased","Urinary ammonia is increased"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Bicarbonate reabsorption is decreased\\n\\nIn the given scenario, the ABG analysis report is suggestive of metabolic acidosis and the false statement regarding the renal compensation in this patient is bicarbonate reabsorption is decreased.\\n\\nThe ABG analysis report for the patient shows a pH of 7.2 and HCO3- level of 15 mEq/L, indicating a low pH (below the normal range of 7.35-7.45) and decreased plasma bicarbonate (below the normal range of 22-28 mEq/L), which suggests metabolic acidosis. In response, the kidneys increase bicarbonate reabsorption as a compensatory mechanism for the acidosis.\\n\\nIn metabolic acidosis, the kidney performs the following\xa0compensatory mechanisms in order to bring back the pH to the normal range (the aim is to increase the acidity in urine and lower the acidity in the blood):\\n\\n• The proximal tubule reabsorbs all the filtered HCO3- (as any loss of HCO3- is equal to the addition of an equimolar amount of H+).\\n• The collecting duct excretes the daily H+ load (loss of H+ is equal to the addition of an equimolar amount of HCO3-) primarily in the form of urinary ammonia.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption a : Hydrogen ion secretion is increased by collecting duct , because loss of H+ is equal to the addition of an equimolar amount of bicarbonate.\\n\\nOption c : Urine acidity is increased because of excretion of H+ ions.\\n\\nOption d : Urine ammonia is increased because the collecting duct excretes the daily H+ load, primarily in the form of urinary ammonia.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":29,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 55-year-old male patient presenting with mild chest pain is admitted in the hospital. A cardiac MRI reveals the findings shown in the image below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":["Apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Takatsubo cardiomyopathy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy\\n\\nThe presence of\xa0spade-like deformity of the left ventricle in cardiac MRI points towards a diagnosis of apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.\\n\\nApical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy,\xa0also called Yamaguchi syndrome,\xa0is characterized by hypertrophy of the myocardium, predominantly in the left ventricular apex. It was first described in the Japanese population and is uncommon in other populations. The symptoms are milder and the\xa0risk of sudden cardiac death is lower than the traditional septal form of HCM. The ECG shows giant T wave inversions in apical precordial leads.\\n\\nThe given MRI image shows apical hypertrophy & obliteration of the LV apical cavity at end-diastole, with a typical spade-like configuration as shown below.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption a : Takotsubo cardiomyopathy presents as acute anterior myocardial infarction, with acute chest pain or shortness of breath. It occurs soon after a high catecholamine surge and is seen predominantly in postmenopausal women. \xa0Cardiac catheterization shows normal coronaries. The LV appears as a round ampulla, resembling the Japanese octopus pot called takotsubo pot. Imaging reveals apical left ventricular ballooning due to myocardial stunning.\\n\\nOption b : Restrictive cardiomyopathy is characterized by diastolic dysfunction of a non-dilated ventricle with, mildly decreased contractility and ejection fraction. They often present with right-sided heart symptoms like edema, ascites, and abdominal discomfort. A fourth heart sound is predominately heard than a third heart sound and is often associated with atrial fibrillation. JVP shows rapid y descent and may increase with inspiration.\\n\\nOption c : Dilated cardiomyopathy presents with symptoms and signs of left heart failure or biventricular heart failure. Patients show cardiomegaly, and a third heart sound can be auscultated. The most frequent causes are alcoholism, myocarditis, chemotherapy, and endocrinopathies. Echocardiogram shows left ventricular dilation and dysfunction.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/84dd777d7a73963bcf93.png"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/0860ddfbed5acfb2a363.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/c5987084f8313aa7b486.jpg"}]},{"n":30,"subject":"Medicine","text":"An 11-year-old boy was brought to the outpatient clinic with intentional tremors and poor scholastic performance. His sister has similar complaints. On examination, hepatomegaly is seen. The eye finding is shown in the image. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":["Wilson\'s disease","Huntington\'s chorea","Glutaric aciduria","Hepatitis A"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Wilson\'s disease\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario of a child with\xa0neurological symptoms (intentional tremors and\xa0poor scholastic performance) and liver disease (icterus and hepatomegaly)\xa0is suggestive of Wilson\'s disease.\\n\\nWilson\'s disease is an\xa0autosomal-recessive\xa0condition caused by mutations in the\xa0ATP7B\xa0gene, which encodes for the ATPase that removes copper, preventing its accumulation in the liver and brain. Liver disease is the most common manifestation in children which can present as\xa0hepatomegaly\xa0(cirrhosis in advanced stages) with or without splenomegaly, subacute or chronic hepatitis, and acute liver failure. Neurologic features consist of dysarthria, dystonia, rigidity, tremors, and choreiform movements due to copper deposition in the basal ganglia. Psychiatric symptoms encompass personality changes, such as irritability and anger, as well as depression and anxiety.\\n\\nKayser–Fleischer (KF) rings (deposition of copper in Descement\'s membrane) are present in 95% of patients with neurological symptoms\xa0and\xa0absent in half of the young patients with liver-related Wilson disease. After treatment KF ring resolves.\xa0Coomb\'s negative hemolytic anemia may also be seen.\xa0Hemolytic anemia can result from the release of copper into the circulation from the liver. Additional manifestations of Wilson\'s disease comprise renal tubular acidosis, delayed puberty, and osteoarthritis.\\n\\nDiagnosis is made by the presence of a\xa0KF ring and\xa0decreased levels of\xa0serum copper and ceruloplasmin.\xa0Increased 24-hour urine copper excretion is seen. Hepatic copper content greater than 250 μg/g dry weight measured by liver biopsy is the gold standard.\\n\\nCopper intake should be restricted to <1 mg/day. Initial treatment involves copper-chelating agents D-penicillamine and trientine with concurrent use of pyridoxine.\xa0Penicillamine may worsen neurologic symptoms, so trientine is an alternative.\xa0Ammonium tetrathiomolybdate is another chelating agent being investigated for patients with neurologic disease.\\n\\nZinc is used in presymptomatic patients\xa0due to its ability to impair the absorption of copper.\xa0Liver transplantation is considered in advanced stages.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Huntington\'s chorea\xa0is a\xa0trinucleotide\xa0(CAG)\xa0repeat disorder\xa0with an\xa0autosomal dominant\xa0mode of inheritance. It is characterized by involuntary movements known as\xa0chorea.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Glutaric aciduria is an autosomal recessive disease that manifests in the 1st year of life. It is caused by a deficiency of the enzyme glutaryl-CoA dehydrogenase. Clinical features are hypotonia and jitteriness at birth, progressive hyperkinetic movements (dystonia, choreoathetosis), and may have seizures.\\n\\nOption D: Hepatitis A is a viral infection transmitted by the\xa0fecal-oral\xa0route, which is the commonest, parenteral route, and also by sexual transmission.\xa0The onset of\xa0jaundice\xa0is preceded by symptoms like\xa0nausea,\xa0vomiting,\xa0anorexia, and mild\xa0fever.\xa0Hepatitis A\xa0resolves completely\xa0but a relapse may occur in a few cases.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/f7d7174f0aa614ed209d.jpg"]},{"n":31,"subject":"Medicine","text":"An intern has a needle-prick injury while establishing an IV line on a patient in the casualty. If the intern were to get infected with hepatitis B, what is the first marker to rise in an acute infection?","options":["Anti HBs","HBsAg","HBeAg","Anti HBc"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. HBsAg\\n\\nThe first virologic marker to rise in acute hepatitis B is HBsAg. HBsAg is the surface antigen of the hepatitis B virus (HBV).\\n\\nIt is detectable in serum within 1–12 weeks.\xa0Usually, HBsAg levels go down in 1–2 months after the onset of jaundice. If it persists beyond 6 months, it indicates chronic hepatitis.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption A: Anti HBs is positive if the patient is recovering from hepatitis B infection or in case of immunisation.\\n\\nOption C: The presence of HBeAg indicates active replication of HBV. Therefore, HBeAg is the qualitative marker of infectivity.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0The most reliable indicator of recent HBV infection is IgM anti-HBc in serum. It is demonstrable in the serum about 1-2 weeks after the appearance of HBsAg, and may sometimes persist in the serum even longer than Anti-HBs antibody.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":32,"subject":"Medicine","text":"You are posted in your medicine ward and your consultant asks you to prescribe an ultra-short-acting insulin to a patient. Inside the drug tray, you notice the following drugs. Identify the drug that you will not give to the patient.","options":["Aspart","Glargine","Glulisine","Lispro"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Glargine\\n\\nGlargine is a long-acting insulin. Hence, it will not be given to the patient in this scenario.\\n\\nAspart, Lispro and Glulisine are all ultra-short-acting insulins.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":33,"subject":"Medicine","text":"Identify the pathology from the given flow-volume loop:","options":["Fixed distal airway obstruction","Fixed central airway obstruction","Variable intrathoracic obstruction","Variable extrathoracic obstruction"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Fixed central airway obstruction\\n\\nThe pathology associated with given flow volume loop is fixed central airway obstruction.\\n\\nThe given flow-volume curve shows flattening of both the inspiratory and expiratory limbs. The curve denotes airflow limitation during both inspiration and expiration. Examples of fixed central airway obstruction include tracheal stenosis (caused by intubation) and circular tracheal tumors.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: The image given below shows a concave upward, pattern in the expiratory limb (scooped-out or coved) pattern seen in lower airway obstruction. Examples include asthma, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, bronchiectasis, and bronchiolitis.\\n\\nOption C: The image below shows airflow limitation and flattening in the expiratory limb of the loop. It is seen in variable, nonfixed intrathoracic obstruction.\\n\\nExamples: intrathoracic airway, bronchogenic cysts, or tracheal lesions.\\n\\nOption D: The image below shows airflow limitation and flattening in the inspiratory limb of the loop. It is seen in nonfixed variable extrathoracic obstruction.\\n\\nExamples: vocal cord paralysis, extrathoracic tracheomalacia, polychondritis, etc.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/f2ba86c3f4e417603dce.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/31f1f48ea9e3646ea374.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/abfb75d12e74aca405a0.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/f1a675c47213887b2a90.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a7dabbe39cd5dcee5e80.png"}]},{"n":34,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A patient presented with massive abdominal swelling. Paracentesis fluid was sent for lab analysis and results show serum protein to be 3g/dL and ascitic fluid protein to be 1g/dL. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Tuberculosis","Congestive heart failure","Late Budd Chiari syndrome","Early Budd Chiari syndrome"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Late Budd Chiari syndrome\\n\\nThe likely diagnosis in the given clinical scenario with serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) >1.1g/dL and ascitic fluid protein <2.5 g/dL is late Budd Chiari syndrome.\\n\\nSerum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) can be used to differentiate between ascites due to portal hypertension and non-portal hypertensive ascites. It represents the pressure within the hepatic sinusoids and correlates with the hepatic venous pressure gradient.\\n\\nIt is given by the formula:\xa0SAAG = Serum albumin level - Ascitic fluid albumin level.\\n\\nInterpretation of SAAG:\\n\\n• SAAG ≥1.1 g/dL indicates that the ascites is due to increased pressure in the hepatic sinusoids. It reflects the presence of portal hypertension.\\n• SAAG <1.1 g/dL indicates that the ascites is not related to portal hypertension. It may be due to infection or malignancy.\\n\\nIn cases with high-SAAG (≥1.1), ascitic protein level analysis further helps in identifying the etiology:\\n\\n• ≥2.5 g/dL indicates that the hepatic sinusoids are normal and are allowing the passage of protein into the ascites as in early Budd-Chiari syndrome.\\n• <2.5 g/dL indicates that the hepatic sinusoids have been damaged and scarred and no longer allow passage of protein as in late Budd-Chiari syndrome\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption a :\xa0Congestive heart failure, has SAAG ≥1.1, with Ascitic protein >2.5g/dL, indicating the hepatic sinusoids are normal and are allowing the passage of protein into the ascites.\\n\\nOption b :\xa0Early Budd-Chiari syndrome, has SAAG ≥1.1, with Ascitic protein >2.5g/dL,\xa0indicating the hepatic sinusoids are normal and are allowing the passage of protein into the ascites.\\n\\nOption d :\xa0Tuberculosis\xa0is associated with SAAG <1.1g/dL indicating that the ascites is not related to portal hypertension.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":35,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A patient recently diagnosed with chronic lymphocytic leukemia starts fludarabine and later develops vomiting, abdominal pain, hematuria, and signs of kidney dysfunction along with electrolyte abnormalities and elevated lactate dehydrogenase. Which condition has he likely developed?","options":["Tumor lysis syndrome","Chronic kidney disease","Urate arthropathy","Nephrotoxic nephropathy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Tumor lysis syndrome\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario and lab findings, are suggestive of tumor lysis syndrome.\\n\\nThe patient has been started on fludarabine, a cytotoxic drug to treat chronic lymphocytic leukemia. The cytotoxic drug causes lysis of tumor cells, leading to hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, hypocalcemia, and hyperuricemia. The uric acid crystals and calcium phosphate precipitate in the tubules and cause damage to the epithelium, subsequently causing renal failure.\\n\\nPatients with impaired renal function, high tumor burden, and elevated lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) level are more likely to develop tumor lysis syndrome.\\n\\nManagement is by aggressive hydration and administration of\xa0allopurinol at the same time as fludarabine are used to reduce the incidence of tumor lysis syndrome.\xa0Febuxostat, a non-selective xanthine oxidase inhibitor, can be used to treat hyperuricemia. It does not require dose calculations and can be used in patients with mildly decreased renal function.\xa0Rasburicase, a recombinant urate oxidase, may also be used in patients with renal failure.\xa0Hemodialysis can be done in severe cases not responding to treatment.\\n\\nOther options:\\nOption B - Chronic kidney disease would have a long-term history of impaired kidney function. This patient’s kidney dysfunction is of recent onset. In addition, his laboratory values are more suggestive of tumor lysis syndrome.\\n\\nOption C - Urate arthropathy (gouty arthritis) is not likely in this patient as he has no joint involvement.\\n\\nOption D - Nephrotoxic nephropathy is unlikely in this patient. Fludarabine is a purine nucleotide analog that causes myelosuppression, immunosuppression, fever, myalgia, and arthralgias.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":36,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A newborn baby presented with yellowish discoloration of the skin. Investigations revealed markedly elevated direct bilirubin levels. Which of the following conditions should be suspected in the newborn?","options":["Hemolytic disease of the newborn","Biliary atresia","Breast milk jaundice","Physiological jaundice of the newborn"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Biliary atresia\\n\\nThe raised direct bilirubin levels indicate conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, which is suggestive of\xa0biliary atresia.\\n\\nBiliary atresia is the disorder that causes destruction or absence of bile ducts in a newborn. It leads to obstruction to the flow of bile thereby causing leaking of conjugated bilirubin into the circulation. It\xa0may also be termed as noncystic obliterative cholangiopathy. It is a disease of the intra and extrahepatic bile ducts of unknown etiology.\\n\\nThe classification of biliary atresia with the regions involved is as follows:\\n\\n• Type I - Common bile duct.\\n• Type IIa - Common hepatic duct.\\n• Type IIb - Common bile duct, hepatic duct, cystic duct.\\n• Type III (most common type) - Common bile duct, hepatic duct, cystic duct; no anastomosable ducts at porta hepatis.\\n\\nClinical features include persistent jaundice, clay-colored stools, and hepatomegaly. Untreated newborns will show signs of liver cirrhosis and failure (ascites, portal hypertension, and failure to thrive).\\n\\nLaboratory evaluation will show elevated levels of direct and indirect bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase, and gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase. Ultrasound will show hypoplastic or absent gall bladder and the triangular cord sign, hyperechoic structure at porta hepatitis. The definitive diagnosis is by intraoperative cholangiography. Preoperative percutaneous transhepatic cholecystocholangiography combined with liver biopsy in cholestatic infants has a sensitivity of 100% and a specificity of 93% for diagnosing biliary atresia.\xa0Treatment is surgery via roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy for type I and Kasai procedure or liver transplantation for type II and III.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption b :\xa0Physiological jaundice of the newborn is because of the immature liver of the newborn that is not able to conjugate the excess bilirubin produced after birth. It leads to unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.\\n\\nOption c : Breast milk Jaundice , is associated with breast milk which may contain certain factors that inhibit the conjugation of bilirubin leading to unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.\\n\\nOption d : Hemolytic disease of the newborn causes unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia because of the excess production of bilirubin due to hemolysis.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":37,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 25-year-old patient presents with loss of pain and temperature sensation in both upper limbs. An MRI is done which is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Spinal epidural abscess","Vitamin B12 deficiency","Syringomyelia","Spinal cord infarction"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Syringomyelia\\n\\nThe most likely diagnosis based on the given clinical scenario and the image showing a hyperintense area in the spinal cord is syringomyelia.\\n\\nSyringomyelia is a progressive disease of the spinal cord in which there is an enlarging cavity in the cord. Syrinx cavities are acquired cavities of the cord in necrosed areas secondary to trauma, myelitis, and spinal cord tumors. It usually presents in adolescence or early adulthood with central cord symptoms.\\n\\nMost patients present with regional loss of pain and temperature of the upper limbs. Touch and vibration senses are intact. The loss of sensation has a \'cape distribution\' as it affects the nape of the neck, shoulders, and upper arms. Eventually, expansion of the cavity may lead to compression of the long tracts. This results in spasticity, weakness, bowel and bladder dysfunction, and Horner\'s syndrome.\\n\\nThere is no satisfactory treatment for this condition.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Spinal epidural abscess presents with fever, pain, and progressive weakness. The pain may be radicular. There would be elevated white cell count and inflammatory markers. The image below shows a spinal epidural abscess compressing the spinal cord.\\n\\nOption B: Vitamin B12 deficiency leads to subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord. Patients present with paresthesias in the hands and feet and progressive ataxic weakness. They would be areflexic due to peripheral neuropathy. The image below shows a hyperintensity in the posterior part of the spinal cord due to the involvement of the posterior columns.\\n\\nOption D: Spinal cord infarction occurs due to infarction of one of the three arteries (anterior spinal artery and two posterior spinal arteries.) Infarction of the anterior spinal artery leads to quadriplegia or paraplegia, loss of pain and temperature, and sparing of vibration and position sense. Posterior spinal artery involvement leads to a loss of posterior column functions.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/56e89ef2fb6f21b15582.png"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/804ac61a1814df5afe78.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/f3be0fa1084b51a94479.jpg"}]},{"n":38,"subject":"Medicine","text":"Match the following:\\n\\n1. First-degree heart block\\n2. Mobitz type 1 heart block\\n3. Mobitz type 2 heart block\\n4. Complete heart block","options":["1- d, 2- b, 3- a, 4- c","1- a, 2- c, 3- b, 4- d","1- b, 2- d, 3- c, 4- a","1- c, 2- a, 3- d, 4- b"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 1- c, 2- a, 3- d, 4- b\\n\\nThe correctly matched pairs are\xa01- c, 2- a, 3- d, and 4- b.\\n\\nThe different types of heart blocks are:\\n\\nFirst Degree Heart Block: It occurs due to slowing of conduction through the AV junction.\xa0It is characterized by prolonged PR > 0.2s and equal RR intervals.\\n\\nSecond Degree Heart Block: It occurs due to the\xa0intermittent failure of electrical impulse conduction from the atrium to the ventricle. It is characterized by a missed QRS. It is of two types:\\n\\n• Mobitz type 1/ Wenckebach\xa0occurs due to the decremental conduction of electrical impulses in the AV node. It is characterized by a progressive PR elongation leading to an eventual drop in the QRS complex. This may be seen in young athletes.\\n\\n• Mobitz type 2\xa0occurs in the distal or infra-His conduction system and is often associated with intraventricular conduction delays.\xa0 It is characterized by an\xa0intermittent failure of conduction of the p wave\xa0without any change in the preceding impulses.\xa0On ECG there is a fixed PR interval and missed QRS.\\n\\nThird Degree Heart Block/Complete Heart Block\\n\\nIt occurs when none of the impulses from the atria are conducted to the ventricles. It is characterized by\\n\\n• Escape rhythm\\n• RR equal, PP equal\\n• P waves > QRS waves\\n• No relationship between P & QRS","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/d41be34ca04a4aa68b1a.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/4424dfeb7b58a1a8ddb0.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/9e8ecdb1bbfac8779ecf.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/72bd55897bd68ffe794d.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/fc652299a368c848f7e9.png"}]},{"n":39,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 60-year-old male patient with a known history of CKD presents with symptoms of dehydration, thirst, anorexia, and hypotonia. After further evaluation, he is diagnosed with type 1 RTA. Which of the following is not a characteristic feature of type 1 RTA?\\n\\nCKD-Chronic kidney disease\\n\\nRTA-Renal tubular acidosis","options":["Urinary pH after acid loading is <5.5","Nephrocalcinosis","Renal rickets","Hypokalemia"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Urinary pH after acid loading is <5.5\\n\\nUrinary pH after acid loading is <5.5\xa0is not a feature of\xa0type-1 RTA.\\n\\nIn RTA type I, there is defect in DCT(distal convoluted tubule) which is a site of acidification of urine, therefore minimum pH after acid loading will always be more than 5.5.\\n\\nDue to defective DCT, there is bicarbonaturia but fractional excretion of HCO3- is less than 10-15 % ( less than RTA type II). The loss of HCO3- creates negative charge in lumen of tubules and to maintain electroneutrality more potassium ion (a positive charge) is secreted in DCT, therefore leading to hypokalemia\xa0(Option a).\\n\\nHypercalciuria which occurs because of metabolic acidosis (as it causes bone resorption leading to hypercalcemia and then hypercalciuria) causes renal rickets (Option b).\\n\\nCo-existance of hypercalciuria and hypocitraturia (beacuse of increased cell citrate utilization followed by intracellular acidosis) leads to Nephrocalcinosis (Option d).","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":40,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 40-year-old patient with alcohol addiction presents with loss of appetite, jaundice, and easy bruisability. The following abnormality is noted on examination. This nail change occurs due to:","options":["Inflammatory Bowel Disease","Chronic iron deficiency","Hypoalbuminemia","Hyperthyroidism"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Hypoalbuminemia\\n\\nThe nail change seen in the given image is leukonychia totalis\xa0and it occurs due to\xa0hypoalbuminemia.\\n\\nLeukonychia is the opaque whitish discoloration of the nail. It is a prominent feature of hypoalbuminemic states. The given clinical scenario is suggestive of liver cirrhosis which is one of the causes of leukonychia.\\n\\nLeukonychia is seen in the following conditions :\\n\\n• Dietary deficiency: Protein-energy malnutrition, pellagra, zinc deficiency.\\n• Drugs: Cytotoxic agents, Steroids, Retinoids, Pilocarpine.\\n• Dermatological pathology: Alopecia areata, erythema multiforme, exfoliative dermatitis, and SLE.\\n• Systemic diseases: Liver cirrhosis, renal failure, and cardiac failure.\\n• Miscellaneous: Trauma, malignancy, and toxins (arsenic, fluoride) and idiopathic condition.\\n\\nNote: The leukonychia secondary to arsenic poisoning is termed leukonychia striata or Aldrich–Mees lines, as shown in the image below.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption a : Inflammatory bowel disease is associated with clubbing.\\n\\nOption c : Chronic iron deficiency is associated with Koilonychia.\\n\\nOption d : Hyperthyroidism is associated with oncholysis.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/edfaf7df5d283b8022f7.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/84e6eb5deb4103a3538a.jpg"}]},{"n":41,"subject":"Medicine","text":"Which of the following clinical characteristics is not associated with MEN1 syndrome?","options":["Medullary carcinoma thyroid","Foregut carcinoid","Parathyroid adenoma","Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Medullary carcinoma thyroid\\n\\nMedullary thyroid carcinoma is not associated with MEN-1 syndrome. It is associated with MEN-2 syndrome.\\n\\nMEN-1 or Wermer’s syndrome is characterized by the triad of tumours affecting the parathyroid gland, pancreatic islets, and anterior pituitary. In addition, adrenocorticotropic tumours, carcinoid tumours usually of the foregut, meningiomas, facial angiofibroma, collagenomas, and lipomas may also occur in some patients with MEN1.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption B: Foregut carcinoid is associated with MEN-1 syndrome.\\n\\nOption C: Parathyroid adenoma is associated with MEN-1 syndrome.\\n\\nOption D: Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours, most commonly gastrinoma, are associated with MEN-1 syndrome.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":42,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A patient who suffered from myocardial infarction four days ago now complains of dyspnea. On auscultation, a pansystolic murmur is heard, which is loudest at the tricuspid area. Which of the following complications is likely to have occurred in this patient?","options":["Ventricular free wall rupture","Ventricular septal perforation","Papillary muscle rupture","Ventricular aneurysm"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Ventricular septal perforation\\n\\nIn a patient developing pansystolic murmur which is loudest in tricuspid area, four days following a myocardial infarction, a diagnosis of ventricular septal perforation is most likely.\\n\\nPansystolic (or holosystolic) murmurs begin with S1 and continue through systole to S2. They are usually indicative of chronic mitral or tricuspid valve regurgitation or a ventricular septal defect. Post myocardial infarction ventricular septal rupture produces a pansystolic murmur which is loudest at the mid- to lower-left sternal border and accompanied by a systolic thrill at the left sternal border. This should be differentiated from early systolic murmur, which is seen in acute mitral regurgitation which develops following papillary muscle rupture (option D). This early systolic murmur is best heard at the apical impulse.\\n\\nBoth ventricular septal perforation and papillary muscle rupture mostly occur in the first week following myocardial infarction. A new onset murmur in a patient with myocardial infarction is an indication for transthoracic echocardiography because it allows precise characterization of the pathology behind the murmur.\\n\\nVentricular aneurysm (option A) occurs because of outward bulging of the ventricular wall during contraction (dyskinesia). It is not associated with any mumurs, but it can lead to the formation of mural thrombus and embolism. Ventricular free wall rupture (option B) causes cardiac tamponade and often death.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":43,"subject":"Medicine","text":"Which of the following cytokines is produced by adipocytes?","options":["Tumor necrosis factor α","Interleukin - 6","Plasminogen activator inhibitor-1","All of the above"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. All of the above\\n\\nThe cytokines produced by adipocytes include all the following, tumor necrosis factor alpha, interleukin-6, plasminogen activator inhibitor-1.\\n\\nAlthough adipocytes have been regarded as storage depot of fats in general, they are also considered as\xa0endocrine cells that release numerous molecules in a regulated manner, as given in the image below.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/57624f1d59487d9f06bd.jpg"}]},{"n":44,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 35-year-old woman with a history of sarcoidosis presents with skin lesions shown in the image below. Identify the condition.","options":["Lupus vulgaris","Lupus pernio","Heliotrope rash","Erysipelas"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Lupus pernio\\n\\nThe given condition is\xa0lupus pernio. Lupus pernio is a cutaneous manifestation of sarcoidosis. It involves the bridge of the nose, the area beneath the eyes, and the cheeks.\\n\\nSarcoidosis is a granulomatous inflammatory condition. The most common organs affected are the lungs, liver, eyes, and skin. It is more common in women. Though Asteroid and Schaumann bodies are characteristic of sarcoidosis, they are not pathognomonic of sarcoidosis because these bodies can be seen in other granulomatous diseases like tuberculosis, etc.\\n\\nIn sarcoidosis, there is an increase in CD4+ T-cells (increased CD4+:CD8+ ratio). As a result, cytokines like IL-2, TNF, IL-8, and IFN-gamma will increase due to which granulomas form in multiple organs. They are classically described as non-caseating granulomas and are formed of epithelioid macrophages with giant cells with no central necrosis, unlike tubercular granulomas.\\n\\nDermatological features include maculopapular lesions, keloid formation, and subcutaneous nodules. Lupus pernio refers to the skin involvement of the bridge of the nose, periocular region, and cheeks and points to chronic disease.\\n\\nThe image below shows the characteristic non-caseating granuloma.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A- Lupus vulgaris is a manifestation of cutaneous tuberculosis. It is characterized by reddish-brown papules and slow-growing plaques with elevated borders and central atrophy as shown in the image below.\\n\\nOption C- Heliotrope rash presents as violaceous lesions around eyelids. It is seen in dermatomyositis.\\n\\nThe image of heliotrope rash is shown below.\\n\\nOption D- \xa0Erysipelas is an infection of the superficial epidermis. It presents as an erythematous raised lesion with sharply demarcated edges that differentiate it from the surrounding skin. The given image shows erysipelas.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/034e7b22cd71aded1e57.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/fcc31ce7de435c1589af.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/1a55ad6fa99756236e57.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/ca37424da3ee9e635103.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/10f4cd0943a60ef399b1.jpg"}]},{"n":45,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 20-year-old IV drug user presents with fever. On examination, his temperature is 38.5\xb0C, and fundoscopic examination reveals spots over the fundus. Laboratory investigations show a leukocyte count of 20,000/cu mm, and blood cultures have been sent. Infective endocarditis is suspected. How many minor Duke\'s criteria are fulfilled in this case?","options":["3","2","4","1"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 3\\n\\nThis patient, who is an intravenous drug user, with a fever of 38.5\xb0C\xa0and\xa0spots on the fundus, indicating immunologic\xa0phenomena has 3 minor Duke\'s criteria.\\n\\nInfective endocarditis\xa0is associated with bacterial or fungal infection of the valvular or endocardial surface of the heart. The definitive diagnosis can only be established by histological and microbiological examinations of the vegetation.\\n\\nHowever, a diagnostic schema called,\xa0modified Duke criteria,\xa0is usually used for diagnosis. The criteria are based on clinical, laboratory, and echocardiographic findings commonly encountered in patients with endocarditis, as described below.\\n\\nThe diagnosis is made based on the presence of\\n\\n• Two major criteria, or\\n• One major and three minor criteria, or\\n• Five minor criteria.\\n\\nNote: There has been a recent update in the modified Duke\'s criteria, which is given in the pearl below.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":46,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 10 year old girl exhibits rapid, semi-purposeful, graceful, dance-like, involuntary movements. This is an example of_________.","options":["Tremor","Myoclonus","Chorea","Athetosis"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Chorea\\n\\nThe given scenario of rapid, semi-purposeful, graceful, dance-like, involuntary movements are seen in chorea.\\n\\nChorea is characterized by involuntary, purposeless, jerky movements that mainly affect the head and upper limbs. The characteristic darting movements in the tongue are visible. The choreic movements are caused by the destruction of D2 neurons of the neostriatum. The neostriatum consists of the caudate nucleus and putamen.\\n\\nHyperkinetic Movement Disorders:\\n\\nTremor\\n\\nRhythmic oscillation of a body part due to intermittent muscle contractions.\\n\\nDystonia\\n\\nInvoluntary, sustained or repeated muscle contractions often associated with twisting movements and abnormal posture.\\n\\nAthetosis\\n\\nSlow, distal, writhing, involuntary movements with a propensity to affect arms and hands.\\n\\nChorea\\n\\nRapid, semi-purposeful, graceful, dance-like, non patterned, involuntary movements involving distal or proximal muscle groups.\\n\\nMyoclonus\\n\\nSudden, brief (<100ms), jerk-like arrhythmic muscle twitches.\\n\\nTics\\n\\nBrief, repeated, stereotyped muscle contractions that can often be suppressed for a short time.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption A: Tremor is rhythmic oscillation of a body part due to intermittent muscle contractions.\\n\\nOption B: Myoclonus is sudden, brief (<100ms), jerk-like arrhythmic muscle twitches.\\n\\nOption D: Athetosis is slow, distal, writhing, involuntary movements with a propensity to affect arms and hands.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":47,"subject":"Medicine","text":"All of the following are contraindications of LP except?","options":["Raised intracranial tension","Meningitis","Local skin infection at the site of LP","Bleeding diathesis"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Meningitis\\n\\nThe presence of skin infection near the site of the LP increases the risk of carrying the infection into the CSF with the LP needle. Thus, infection at the potential LP site is a contraindication to performing a lumbar puncture at that area.\\n\\nPerhaps the more worrisome contraindication to lumbar puncture is the suspicion of increased intracranial pressure (ICP) due to a cerebral mass lesion. In the presence of a potential brain tumor, cerebral hemorrhage, cavernous sinus thrombosis, brain abscesses, epidural or subdural hematomas , patients are at increased risk of deteriorating neurologically with LP. As the space-occupying lesion grows, ICP rises. When lumbar puncture is performed in these patients, a low-pressure shunt is formed at the site of LP where CSF can escape.\\n\\nAs the CSF pressure drops in the spinal column, CSF and brain mass may then shift towards the low-pressure outlet (the LP site). This may lead to either trans-tentorial or uncal herniation and acute neurological deterioration.\\n\\nPatients with increased ICP from mass lesions often display decreased levels of consciousness, focal neurological signs or papilledema on physical exam. Any of these findings make lumbar puncture contraindicated until further evaluation can be undertaken.\\n\\nAs with most invasive procedures, uncorrected coagulopathy is a contraindication to LP. This includes those on heparin, coumadin, or with clotting defects such as disseminated intravascular coagulation, hemophilia or thrombocytopenia.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":48,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A 68-year-old man who underwent coronary stenting for an acute STEMI presents at a follow-up visit with pain in his toes and discoloration of both feet, as shown in the image. His creatinine levels are also elevated. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Atheroembolism","Aortic dissection","Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome","Sharp syndrome"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Atheroembolism\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of\xa0atheroembolism. It\xa0is\xa0also called cholesterol embolism or blue toe syndrome.\\n\\nAtheroembolism is a condition in which multiple small deposits of fibrin, platelets, and cholesterol debris embolize from proximal atherosclerotic lesions or aneurysmal sites.\\n\\nThe typical presentation is a patient with ST-elevation myocardial infarction presenting with features of limb ischemia (cyanotic discoloration of the toes with pain and tenderness) following an intraarterial procedure (angioplasty).\\n\\nDigital vascular occlusion may result in ischemia and the \\"blue toe\\" syndrome; digital necrosis and gangrene may develop.\xa0Localized areas of tenderness, pallor, and livedo reticularis occur at sites of emboli. Since the atheroemboli\xa0tend to lodge in the small vessels\xa0of the limbs and may not occlude the large vessels,\xa0distal pulses are usually palpable.\\n\\nLarge protruding aortic atheromas are a source of emboli that may lead to limb ischemia, as well as stroke and renal insufficiency.\xa0Skin or muscle biopsy may demonstrate cholesterol crystals.\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption a : Sharp syndrome, also known as mixed connective tissue disease, is an autoimmune condition with features of SLE, systemic sclerosis, and polymyositis. Clinical features include\xa0Raynaud\'s syndrome,\xa0polyarthritis, myositis, swollen hands, and fingers.\\n\\nOption b : Antiphospholipid syndrome (APS) is an autoantibody-mediated acquired thrombophilia characterized by recurrent arterial or venous thrombosis and/or pregnancy morbidity. It primarily affects females. APS may either be primary or secondary to other autoimmune diseases, mainly SLE (secondary). The diagnosis of APS should be seriously considered in cases of thrombosis, cerebral vascular accidents in individuals <55 years of age, or pregnancy morbidity in the presence of livedo reticularis or thrombocytopenia.\\n\\nOption c : Aortic dissection results from a tear in the intimal layer of the aorta and is associated with risk factors such as age, hypertension, pregnancy, and cocaine use. Patients typically present with severe tearing chest or interscapular pain. Contrast-enhanced Computed Tomography (CECT) is the preferred imaging modality for stable patients, whereas transesophageal echocardiography is preferred for unstable patients.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/c239398d290ecc54cd3b.jpg"]},{"n":49,"subject":"Medicine","text":"A male patient was brought to the emergency room with complaints of headache, vomiting, and blurring of vision after he consumed some locally produced spirit. Which of the following is used for the treatment of his condition?","options":["Fomepizole","Flumazenil","Diazepam","Naloxone"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Fomepizole\\n\\nThe above clinical scenario is suggestive of\xa0methanol toxicity. Ethanol and fomepizole is used for the treatment of this condition.\\n\\nMethanol poisoning is caused due to ingestion of illicit liquor which contains ethanol adulterated with methanol. Methanol is metabolized to formaldehyde and formic acid by alcohol and aldehyde dehydrogenases, respectively. The metabolites of methanol are responsible for the toxic effects.\\n\\nClinically, patients present with headache, vomiting, abdomen pain, uneasiness, confusion, delirium, and coma. Formic acid specifically damages the retina and optic nerve and results in blurring of vision and blindness.\\n\\nElevated levels of formic acid and other organic acids result in\xa0metabolic acidosis in arterial blood gas analysis(ABG).\\n\\nTreatment of methanol poisoning includes gastric lavage with sodium bicarbonate if the patient is brought within 2 hours of ingesting methanol to combat acidosis. Ethanol infusion since ethanol is preferentially metabolized by alcohol dehydrogenase over methanol and helps by reducing the rate of generation of formaldehyde and formic acid.\\n\\nFomepizole is a competitive inhibitor of alcohol dehydrogenase and the drug of choice for methanol poisoning. Folate therapy with calcium leucovorin reduces blood formate levels by enhancing its oxidation. Hemodialysis is used in severe cases of methanol toxicity.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Flumazenil is the inverse agonist of benzodiazepine receptors and is used for the treatment of benzodiazepine overdose.\\n\\nOption C: Diazepam can be used to manage delirium tremens, or seizures due to alcohol withdrawal.\\n\\nOption D: Naloxone is a competitive opioid antagonist used for the treatment of opioid overdose.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":50,"subject":"Medicine","text":"The chief mediator of anemia of chronic disease is ____.","options":["DMT1","Hemojuvelin","Hepcidin","Ferritin"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Hepcidin\\n\\nThe chief mediator of anemia of chronic disease is hepcidin.\\n\\nAnemia of chronic disease is associated with chronic inflammatory states such as inflammatory bowel disease, rheumatoid arthritis, chronic infections, and malignancies.\\n\\nHepcidin\xa0is a positive acute-phase reactant that increases in chronic inflammatory states. It binds with ferroportin which results in both reduced iron intake in the gut and reduced iron transfer from macrophages to erythroid progenitor cells in the bone marrow. Hence, hepcidin is a\xa0negative regulator\xa0of iron balance.","img":false,"explImg":false}]}')}}]);